Here is an example C++ code snippet that performs linear regression analysis and calculates the slope and y-intercept of a given dataset:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    // Define the dataset
    double x[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    double y[] = {2, 4, 5, 4, 5};
    int n = sizeof(x)/sizeof(x[0]);

    // Calculate the mean of x and y
    double sum_x = 0, sum_y = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum_x += x[i];
        sum_y += y[i];
    }
    double mean_x = sum_x / n;
    double mean_y = sum_y / n;

    // Calculate the slope and y-intercept
    double numerator = 0, denominator = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        numerator += (x[i] - mean_x) * (y[i] - mean_y);
        denominator += pow((x[i] - mean_x), 2);
    }
    double slope = numerator / denominator;
    double y_intercept = mean_y - slope * mean_x;

    // Output the results
    cout << "Slope: " << slope << endl;
    cout << "Y-intercept: " << y_intercept << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define the dataset by storing the independent variable in an array called x and the dependent variable in an array called y. We also calculate the size of the arrays using the sizeof operator.
Next, we calculate the mean of x and y by summing up the values in each array and dividing by the number of elements.
We then calculate the slope and y-intercept using a for loop to iterate through each element in the arrays. We use the formula for the slope and y-intercept, which involves calculating the numerator and denominator of the equation separately.
Finally, we output the results using the cout statement.

