To generate a table that contains only unique characters from a given string using HTML and JavaScript, we can follow the following approach:
1. Create an empty object to store unique characters.
2. Loop through each character in the given string.
3. Check if the character already exists in the object.
4. If the character does not exist, add it to the object and append a new row to the table with the character as the cell value.
5. If the character already exists, do nothing.
6. Continue looping until all characters in the string have been processed.
Here's an example code snippet:
```html
<table id="uniqueCharsTable">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Unique Characters</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
  </tbody>
</table>
```
```javascript
function generateUniqueCharsTable(str) {
  const uniqueChars = {};
  const tbody = document.querySelector("#uniqueCharsTable tbody");
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    const char = str[i];
    if (!uniqueChars[char]) {
      uniqueChars[char] = true;
      const row = document.createElement("tr");
      const cell = document.createElement("td");
      cell.textContent = char;
      row.appendChild(cell);
      tbody.appendChild(row);
    }
  }
}
// Example usage
generateUniqueCharsTable("hello world");
```
In terms of performance, this approach has a time complexity of O(n) where n is the length of the string. This is because we are looping through each character in the string only once. The space complexity is also O(n) because we are storing unique characters in an object.
As for edge cases, this approach should work for any string length and any combination of characters. However, it's important to note that this approach only considers ASCII characters. If the string contains non-ASCII characters, such as emojis or special characters, we would need to modify the code to handle them appropriately.

